  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Tuesday: The upper-level low which brought \ncooler temperatures and breezy winds to the Inland Northwest \ncontinues to shift southward through the day on Monday as an \nupper-level ridge begins to nose into the Pacific Northwest. \nPrecipitation wrapping around the northern periphery of this low\nbrings light rain showers to southeast Washington and the \nsouthern Idaho Panhandle through Monday morning. As the ridge \nshifts inland, winds will decrease significantly along with \nclearing skies. This will allow temperatures Monday night to \nplummet across the Inland Northwest with a 100% chance of \ntemperatures 32F and below across the northern valleys, \nWashington and Idaho Palouse, and the central Idaho Panhandle \nvalleys. Chances decrease to a 60-80% chance across the Columbia\nBasin into the Spokane area, resulting in moderate to high \nconfidence for freezing temperatures. Lower elevation areas in \nthe Okanogan Valley and the L-C Valley have a 20-30% chance for \nfreezing temperatures, but a 90-100% chance of temperatures \nbelow 36F. So while confidence for a freeze in these areas is \nlower, those still nursing a garden should try to finish it up \ntoday or take protective measures from frosty conditions.\n\nWednesday through Monday: A weak front on Wednesday flattens \nthe ridge temporarily and brings an increase in clouds and a 15%\nchance for showers across the Cascade crest. The ridge \nstrengthens Thursday, first across the west side, allowing a \nthermal trough to build northward west of the Cascades. This \npromotes offshore flow for Thursday and Friday. Temperatures \ntrend warmer through Friday with afternoon temperatures warming \ninto the mid 60s to low 70s by the end of the work week. These \ntemperatures are 8-13 degrees above normal for this time of \nyear. Overnight lows will be chilly though will gradually trend \nwarmer through the week as well. The northern values will \nexperience values in the upper 20s to low 30s with mostly \neverywhere else in the low to mid 30s Wednesday morning. The \nexception is in the Wenatchee area where lows will be in the \n40s.\n\nEnsembles begin to show the next weather system moving into the\nPacific Northwest between late Friday night and Saturday, \nthough there remains differences on the timing and strength with\nmainly mountain showers and breezy winds. This opens the door \nhowever, for additional systems to move into the Pacific \nNorthwest. Ensembles are in solid agreement for a deeper upper-\nlevel trough to move in early next week with a better shot at \nwidespread precipitation and a return of cooler temperatures \nacross the Inland Northwest. Latest National Blend of Models \n(NBM) guidance indicates probabilities for 24 hour precipitation\ntotals exceeding 0.10 inches are highest over the mountains at \n50-60%, decreasing to 30-40% across eastern Washington and Idaho\nPanhandle valleys, and 15-25% for central Washington. /vmt",
